  "presentation_summary": [
    "14-year-old girl presented with 10 days of high fever up to 103 F, chills, vomiting, epigastric pain, yellow skin/eye discoloration, and brown urine for 5 days.",
    "She had arrived from Pakistan 12 days before presentation, and an older sister had been hospitalized with similar symptoms and hepatitis A positivity one month earlier.",
    "She was icteric and febrile but hemodynamically stable; abdominal exam showed epigastric and right upper quadrant tenderness without guarding, hepatosplenomegaly, or lymphadenopathy.",
    "Initial labs showed ALT 1125 U/L, AST 556 U/L, total bilirubin 200.7 umol/L (11.7 mg/dL), direct bilirubin 177.6 umol/L (10.4 mg/dL), ALP 304 U/L, GGT 177 U/L, albumin 31 g/L, and normal CBC/coagulation profile.",
    "HAV IgM was positive. She initially improved and was discharged with supportive instructions.",
    "Seven days later she returned with worsening yellow discoloration involving skin, eyes, nails, and hands, burning feet, mild headache and dizziness; she was stable and afebrile.",
    "Readmission labs showed severe persistent direct hyperbilirubinemia with total bilirubin up to 547.6 umol/L (32.0 mg/dL) and direct bilirubin up to 461.5 umol/L (27.0 mg/dL), while transaminases had fallen near low hundreds or normal range.",
    "Repeat HAV IgM was positive. EBV IgM/IgG and Mycoplasma IgM were also positive, but autoimmune hepatitis tests were negative; ultrasound was normal, MRI/MRCP showed no stones or ductal obstruction, Wilson evaluation was negative, and liver biopsy showed marked cholestasis with mild portal/lobular inflammation and focal bridging fibrosis.",
    "She was treated conservatively without oral steroids and recovered successfully with bilirubin improvement by follow-up."
  ],
